lee (Acme): Hey @support — we started seeing duplicate POSTs to our payment webhook endpoint from calls made through the TS SDK. Customers got charged twice. We're on redwood-ts SDK v0.13.1 in Next.js API routes (serverless), Node 18. Logs show intermittent 'AbortError: The operation was aborted' and then a retry for the same request id. Anyone seen this?

jessica (support): Ugh, sorry you're hitting that. Can you paste the snippet you use to call the SDK and the SDK config (retries, timeout)? Also npm ls undici and node version would help.

lee (Acme): Quick snippet below — simplified. We created an AbortController once and reuse it across requests because we wanted a global timeout.

```
const controller = new AbortController();

export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const resp = await client.generate({
    model: 'redwood-1',
    input: req.body.prompt,
    // accidentally reusing same signal for all requests
    signal: controller.signal
  });
  res.status(200).json(resp);
}
```

We set retries: 2 in SDK config.

matt (eng-sdk): This is a classic signal-reuse race. If you reuse an AbortController across concurrent requests, one request timing out/being aborted will abort the shared signal and kill the others mid-flight. Our retry layer treats those as retriable network failures and will re-send the request. If your upstream POST is not idempotent (no idempotency-key), you can get duplicates.

matt (eng-sdk): Quick guidance: 1) Don't reuse AbortController across concurrent requests. 2) Use per-request AbortController or let the SDK handle timeouts. 3) For POSTs, add an idempotency key header to safely dedupe retries. 4) Check your global fetch polyfill — Next.js serverless sometimes injects a global fetch that wraps undici; double-wrapping can change signal semantics.

jessica (support): Can you try this change and share results? Also paste npm ls undici output if possible.

lee (Acme): Tried a per-request controller and added idempotency header. Code now:

```
export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const idempotency = `acme-${Date.now()}-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2,8)}`;
  const resp = await client.generate({
    model: 'redwood-1',
    input: req.body.prompt,
    signal: controller.signal,
    headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': idempotency }
  });
  res.status(200).json(resp);
}
```

After deploying that change, duplicate charges stopped. No more AbortError logs.

raja (eng-runtime): A couple of extra notes on the undici/fetch angle: if your app bundle or runtime polyfills `globalThis.fetch` with something that internally reuses signals or wraps the original fetch, abort semantics can change. On some serverless platforms a single long-lived worker may end up sharing runtime-level things unexpectedly. If you see aborts that originate from a different place than your controller, check for middleware that sets timeouts (e.g., Vercel's edge timeouts) or any higher-level fetch wrapper.

matt (eng-sdk): We also recommend configuring the SDK retries to be conservative for non-idempotent endpoints: set retries: 0 or 1, and use exponential backoff with jitter if you must retry. Example SDK config snippet:

```
const client = new RedwoodClient({
  apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY,
  retries: 1,
  retryDelay: (attempt) => Math.round(Math.pow(2, attempt) * 100) + Math.floor(Math.random() * 50)
});
```

And for serverless, prefer very short timeouts so you don't accumulate many in-flight requests that can be aborted together.

jessica (support): Thanks — we'll add docs and a troubleshooting note about not reusing AbortController and adding idempotency keys. Also a short checklist for customer-facing support steps: 1) confirm SDK version, 2) check for shared AbortController, 3) validate idempotency headers present for POSTs, 4) npm ls undici and ensure single fetch polyfill, 5) temporary workaround: set retries=0 for the endpoint.

deploy-bot: Applied support checklist to the docs repo and opened a small PR with the example fixes.

lee (Acme): Confirmed fixed for us. Big thanks to the team. Saved a few angry customers. :relieved:
